1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,698
Chapter four: one axis.

2
00:00:02,018 --> 00:00:04,946
We now have three tools: add, fade, and correct.

3
00:00:05,266 --> 00:00:09,447
They look like different ideas, but every rule so far has the same shape.

4
00:00:09,767 --> 00:00:13,629
S t equals S t minus 1 times A t, plus W t.

5
00:00:14,733 --> 00:00:16,230
W t is the write.

6
00:00:16,550 --> 00:00:19,268
It is almost always a scaled v k transpose.

7
00:00:19,588 --> 00:00:23,746
A t is the transition: what happens to the old state before the write.

8
00:00:24,066 --> 00:00:26,284
The methods differ almost only in A t.

9
00:00:26,604 --> 00:00:30,953
So let's line them up, and watch what each transition does to the space of keys.

10
00:00:31,267 --> 00:00:32,477
One detail helps.

11
00:00:32,797 --> 00:00:36,640
A t multiplies the state from the right, so it acts on the key side.

12
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,698
Each column of S belongs to one key direction.

13
00:00:40,018 --> 00:00:43,197
That is why a diagonal A t fades key channels,

14
00:00:43,197 --> 00:00:46,999
and why I minus beta k k transpose edits along one key.

15
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,489
A t equal to the identity is linear attention.

16
00:00:52,809 --> 00:00:54,489
It does nothing to the old state.

17
00:00:54,809 --> 00:00:55,782
It only adds.

18
00:00:56,102 --> 00:00:58,001
A chunk is trained with plain sums.

19
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:04,088
A scalar times the identity, gamma or alpha t, covers RetNet, Lightning and Mamba-2.

20
00:01:04,408 --> 00:01:07,296
It fades everything, equally, in every direction.

21
00:01:07,616 --> 00:01:10,666
A chunk needs a cumulative product of the decays.

22
00:01:10,967 --> 00:01:13,900
A diagonal matrix of decays is GLA.

23
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:16,359
It fades each key channel on its own,

24
00:01:16,359 --> 00:01:19,828
so one direction can shrink fast while another barely moves.

25
00:01:20,148 --> 00:01:23,133
A chunk uses a cumulative sum, in log space.

26
00:01:23,433 --> 00:01:26,405
I minus beta k k transpose is DeltaNet.

27
00:01:26,725 --> 00:01:30,540
It overwrites along one direction, k, and leaves the rest alone.

28
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:35,170
Gated DeltaNet puts a scalar decay in front: fade, then overwrite.

29
00:01:35,490 --> 00:01:40,179
KDA puts a diagonal decay in front: fade per channel, then overwrite.

30
00:01:40,499 --> 00:01:44,023
All three train chunks with the WY or UT form.

31
00:01:44,333 --> 00:01:50,299
RWKV-7 and Comba use the sum form: a diagonal, minus a low rank term.

32
00:01:50,619 --> 00:01:54,726
Their chunks are trained as a general diagonal plus low rank matrix.

33
00:01:55,033 --> 00:01:59,922
And products of reflections, or rotations, as in DeltaProduct and Mamba-3,

34
00:01:59,922 --> 00:02:02,234
can track structure such as parity.

35
00:02:02,554 --> 00:02:05,359
These are trained per sub-step, or as rotations.

36
00:02:06,667 --> 00:02:08,744
Remember the order toy from chapter three.

37
00:02:09,064 --> 00:02:13,066
The same new fact, order 7314 now ships on day 5,

38
00:02:13,066 --> 00:02:16,904
gives a different answer under each transition.

39
00:02:17,224 --> 00:02:18,363
Adding gives 7.

40
00:02:18,683 --> 00:02:21,622
The delta rule gives exactly 5, and keeps 4.

41
00:02:21,942 --> 00:02:28,110
Gated DeltaNet gives 3.3, but fades order 2208 to 3.2.

42
00:02:28,430 --> 00:02:30,756
KDA gives 3.3, and keeps 4.

43
00:02:31,076 --> 00:02:33,678
The sum form gives 3.1 and 3.2.

44
00:02:33,998 --> 00:02:35,917
Same write, different transitions.

45
00:02:37,633 --> 00:02:40,029
Here is why the training column splits in two.

46
00:02:40,349 --> 00:02:42,863
Diagonal transitions commute with each other.

47
00:02:43,183 --> 00:02:46,681
Apply two of them in either order, and you land in the same place.

48
00:02:47,001 --> 00:02:49,660
So a chunk needs only cumulative decays.

49
00:02:49,980 --> 00:02:52,595
Householder-like transitions do not commute.

50
00:02:52,915 --> 00:02:55,195
Swap the order, and the result changes.

51
00:02:55,515 --> 00:02:58,229
So they need the WY or UT rewrite.

52
00:02:59,933 --> 00:03:05,403
The 2026 research models refine the delta rule along this same axis.

53
00:03:05,723 --> 00:03:10,319
They separate how strongly to erase, how strongly to write, and where to erase.

54
00:03:10,639 --> 00:03:13,337
That means per channel erase and write gates,

55
00:03:13,337 --> 00:03:16,155
an erase direction separate from the write key,

56
00:03:16,155 --> 00:03:18,912
or a step size set by an uncertainty estimate.

57
00:03:19,232 --> 00:03:21,671
None of them changes the size of the state.

58
00:03:22,967 --> 00:03:24,669
So think of A t as a dial.

59
00:03:24,989 --> 00:03:26,418
At one end, it does nothing.

60
00:03:26,738 --> 00:03:27,894
Turn it, and it fades.

61
00:03:28,214 --> 00:03:30,308
Turn it further, and it fades per channel.

62
00:03:30,628 --> 00:03:32,408
Then it overwrites along a key.

63
00:03:32,728 --> 00:03:36,415
At the far end, it reflects and rotates, and can track structure.

64
00:03:37,733 --> 00:03:38,531
To recap.

65
00:03:38,851 --> 00:03:43,652
Every rule is S t equals S t minus 1 times A t, plus W t.

66
00:03:43,972 --> 00:03:48,735
The transition decides what the state can do: nothing, fade, fade per channel,

67
00:03:48,735 --> 00:03:50,566
overwrite, or track structure.

68
00:03:51,467 --> 00:03:56,565
Left over: the transition changes how the table is edited, never how big it is.

69
00:03:56,885 --> 00:04:01,413
Next, a second way to read all of these rules: the state as a small model,

70
00:04:01,413 --> 00:04:02,821
trained while it reads.
